So here's my take on India and Hinduism.

In both cases, I think any specific association wth "xindo" should be avoided as Hindi is a language and then of only some states (whether it will succeed as one of the two official languages is open to debate - given the resistance of many non-Hindi-speaking states).  On the other hand associations with 'srito' could be better justified as Vedic culture is the root of both.

That being the case, I would suggest "sritjda" for the Hindu religion and either "la .indias." or "sritgu'e" for India.  I think that "la .barat." may be contentious.  In India, "India" seems to represent modern India, while "Bharat" (a Hindi word anyway) seems to represent the old India, the India of traditions (see http://www.hinduonnet.com/businessline/2001/08/01/stories/040139mn.htm  for more).

By the way, if gejyspa is reading this, what on earth does "jdazerpre" mean?  I haven't been able to find "zerpre' in the gismu list.

mu'o mi'e .andrus.


